<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Review Problems #3, practice version: the same problems  -->
<!-- as review3-online.ptx, but with every <solution> removed and every  -->
<!-- <feedback> reduced to a neutral right/wrong message, so students     -->
<!-- can check their answers without seeing anything about the solutions. -->
<!-- This file is GENERATED-STYLE but maintained by hand: if the problems -->
<!-- in review3-online.ptx change, regenerate or mirror the change here. -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-review3-practice" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Review Problems #3</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="section-jacobian-substitution" text="title"/>,
      <xref ref="section-triple-integrals-spherical" text="title"/>, and
      <xref ref="section-mass-center-of-mass" text="title"/>. Work each
      problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked
      here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every answer box is graded separately, so a box you fill in correctly
      earns its credit even if another box in the same problem is wrong.
      Press <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checking an answer tells you whether it is right or wrong and
      nothing more<mdash/>use the boxes to test yourself. A complete
      worked solution for every problem will be revealed beneath it
      once solutions are posted.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Give exact answers, not decimals.</term> A rounded decimal
          will be marked wrong, even to several places. Type the exact
          value: <c>pi*8^3/2</c> rather than <c>1608.495</c>, and
          <c>4*5/(3*pi)</c> rather than <c>2.122</c>. A whole number or an
          exact fraction may of course be typed as it stands, so
          <c>77</c> and <c>7/3</c> are both fine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>How to type it.</term> Write products with <c>*</c> and
          powers with <c>^</c>, and write functions with parentheses:
          <c>sqrt(s-t)</c>, <c>ln(9)</c>, <c>exp(-4)</c>. Use <c>pi</c> for
          <m>\pi</m>. So <m>\dfrac{u^2}{v}</m> is typed <c>u^2/v</c>, and
          <m>2\pi\left(e^{-1} - e^{-4}\right)</m> is typed
          <c>2*pi*(exp(-1)-exp(-4))</c>. Spaces are ignored.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Any equivalent form counts.</term> Your answer is compared
          to the exact value, not to one particular way of writing it, so
          <c>2*pi*exp(-1)-2*pi*exp(-4)</c> is accepted just as readily as
          <c>2*pi*(exp(-1)-exp(-4))</c>. Do not simplify on our
          account<mdash/>just do not round.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-review3-practice">

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 1.  Area by a non-linear change of variables  (RP3 #1)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-area-change-of-variables">
    <title>Area by a Non-Linear Change of Variables</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="4" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="7" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="aa" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bb" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="one" context="number">
        <de-expression>1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xuv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>u^2/v</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yuv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>v/u</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xu" context="formula">
        <de-expression>2*u/v</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>-u^2/v^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yu" context="formula">
        <de-expression>-v/u^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/u</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/v</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="area" context="number">
        <de-expression>(aa-1)*ln(bb)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the area of the region <m>D</m> in the first quadrant of the
        <m>xy</m>-plane bounded by
      </p>
      <me>
        xy = 1, \qquad xy = <eval obj="aa"/>, \qquad
        xy^2 = 1, \qquad xy^2 = <eval obj="bb"/>.
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Sketch the region <m>D</m>. (Nothing to submit
        for this part<mdash/>the sketch is for your own use in the parts
        that follow.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Using the non-linear change of variables
        <m>u = xy</m> and <m>v = xy^2</m>, find <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> as
        functions of <m>u</m> and <m>v</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x = x(u,v) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xuv" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>y = y(u,v) =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="yuv" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Find the four partial derivatives in the
        Jacobian matrix of this change of variables, and then its
        determinant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="xu" width="12"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="xv" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="yu" width="12"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="yv" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\left| \dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} \right| =</m>
        <fillin name="b7" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part D.</term> Using the change of variables, set up a double
        integral for the area of <m>D</m> in the form
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{c}^{d}\!\int_{p}^{q}</m>
        <fillin name="b8" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="12"/>
        <m>\,du\,dv</m>, where
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b9" mode="math" ansobj="one" width="5"/>,
        <m>d =</m> <fillin name="b10" mode="math" ansobj="bb" width="5"/>,
        <m>p =</m> <fillin name="b11" mode="math" ansobj="one" width="5"/>, and
        <m>q =</m> <fillin name="b12" mode="math" ansobj="aa" width="5"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part E.</term> Evaluate the double integral to find the area of
        <m>D</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Area <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b13" mode="math" ansobj="area" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Dividing <m>v = xy^2</m> by <m>u = xy</m> gives <m>y = v/u</m>, and
        then <m>x = u/y = u^2/v</m>. The Jacobian matrix has entries
        <m>x_u = 2u/v</m>, <m>x_v = -u^2/v^2</m>, <m>y_u = -v/u^2</m>,
        <m>y_v = 1/u</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
        = \frac{2u}{v}\cdot\frac{1}{u}
        - \left(-\frac{u^2}{v^2}\right)\left(-\frac{v}{u^2}\right)
        = \frac{2}{v} - \frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{v}.
      </me>
      <p>
        The four bounding curves are exactly <m>u = 1</m>,
        <m>u = 9</m>, <m>v = 1</m>, <m>v = 36</m>,
        so <m>D</m> becomes a rectangle and
      </p>
      <me>
        \text{Area}
        = \int_1^{36}\!\!\int_1^{9}
          \frac{1}{v}\,du\,dv
        = \left(9 - 1\right)
          \ln\!\left(36\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        Here the four curves are <m>u = 1</m>, <m>u = 9</m>, <m>v = 1</m> and
<m>v = 36</m>, so the area is
<m>(9-1)\ln 36 = 8\ln 36 \approx 28.67</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 2.  Area of a tilted ellipse by a shear  (RP3 #2)                   -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-shear-ellipse-area">
    <title>Area of a Tilted Ellipse by a Shear</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="twoa" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*a</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bcoef" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2+1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>s-a*t</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>t</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="number">
        <de-expression>1</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="area" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the change of variables
        <m>s = x + <eval obj="a"/>y</m>, <m>t = y</m> to find the area of
        the region bounded by
      </p>
      <me>
        x^2 + <eval obj="twoa"/>xy + <eval obj="bcoef"/>y^2 = 1 .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Invert the change of variables.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x = x(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xst" width="12"/>,
        <m>y = y(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="yst" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the Jacobian determinant
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Find the area of the region.
      </p>
      <p>
        Area <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="area" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Inverting the shear gives <m>x = s - 3t</m>,
        <m>y = t</m>, so the Jacobian matrix is
        <m>\begin{pmatrix} 1 &amp; -3 \\ 0 &amp; 1
        \end{pmatrix}</m> with determinant <m>1</m>, and
        <m>dx\,dy = ds\,dt</m>. Completing the substitution,
      </p>
      <me>
        x^2 + 6xy + 10y^2
        = \left(s - 3t\right)^2
        + 6\left(s - 3t\right)t
        + 10t^2
        = s^2 + t^2 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        so the region is the unit disc <m>T</m> and
        <m>\iint_R dx\,dy = \iint_T ds\,dt = \text{Area}(T) = \pi</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 3</m>, and the area is <m>\pi</m> whatever the value of
<m>a</m>: the shear does not change area.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 3.  A double integral under s = y, t = y - x^2  (RP3 #3)            -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-parabolic-strip">
    <title>A Double Integral Under a Parabolic Change of Variables</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="6" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="nega" context="number">
        <de-expression>-a</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>sqrt(s-t)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yst" context="formula">
        <de-expression>s</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/(2*sqrt(s-t))</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="integrand" context="formula">
        <de-expression>1/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*bsq/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use the change of variables <m>s = y</m>, <m>t = y - x^2</m> to
        evaluate <m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dx\,dy</m> over the region
        <m>R</m> in the first quadrant bounded by <m>y = 0</m>,
        <m>y = <eval obj="bsq"/></m>, <m>y = x^2</m>, and
        <m>y = x^2 - <eval obj="a"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Invert the change of variables. (Take the
        positive square root, since <m>R</m> lies in the first quadrant.)
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x = x(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xst" width="14"/>,
        <m>y = y(s,t) =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="yst" width="10"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the Jacobian determinant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="16"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> The region <m>R</m> becomes a rectangle
        <m>T</m> in the <m>st</m>-plane. Give its limits.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>s</m> runs from
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="5"/> to
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="bsq" width="5"/>, and
        <m>t</m> runs from
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="nega" width="5"/> to
        <fillin name="b7" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="5"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part D.</term> After the substitution, the integrand
        <m>x</m> times the Jacobian simplifies to a constant. What is it?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x \left|\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}\right| =</m>
        <fillin name="b8" mode="math" ansobj="integrand" width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part E.</term> Evaluate the integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_R x\,dx\,dy =</m>
        <fillin name="b9" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Inverting gives <m>x = \sqrt{s-t}</m> and <m>y = s</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(s,t)}
        = \begin{vmatrix}
          \frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}} &amp; -\frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}} \\
          1 &amp; 0
          \end{vmatrix}
        = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}} .
      </me>
      <p>
        The boundaries <m>y = x^2</m> and <m>y = x^2 - 4</m>
        become <m>t = 0</m> and <m>t = -4</m>, while
        <m>y = 0</m> and <m>y = 9</m> become <m>s = 0</m>
        and <m>s = 9</m>. The integrand times the Jacobian
        collapses to <m>\sqrt{s-t}\cdot\frac{1}{2\sqrt{s-t}}
        = \frac{1}{2}</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_R x\,dx\,dy
        = \iint_T \frac{1}{2}\,ds\,dt
        = \frac{1}{2}\,\text{Area}(T)
        = \frac{\left(4\right)\left(9\right)}{2}
        = 18 .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 4</m> and <m>b^2 = 9</m>, so the rectangle <m>T</m> is
<m>4 \times 9</m> and the integral equals
<m>\dfrac{(4)(9)}{2} = 18</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 4.  Triple integral over an ellipsoid  (RP3 #4)                     -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-ellipsoid-integral">
    <title>A Triple Integral Over an Ellipsoid</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="c" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="number">
        <de-expression>a*b*c</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>4*a*b*c*pi/5</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Calculate the triple integral
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_{E} \left(\frac{x^2}{<eval obj="a"/>^2}
        + \frac{y^2}{<eval obj="b"/>^2}
        + \frac{z^2}{<eval obj="c"/>^2}\right) dx\,dy\,dz ,
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>E</m> is the region bounded by the ellipsoid
        <m>\dfrac{x^2}{<eval obj="a"/>^2}
        + \dfrac{y^2}{<eval obj="b"/>^2}
        + \dfrac{z^2}{<eval obj="c"/>^2} = 1</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Use the change of variables
        <m>x = <eval obj="a"/>u</m>, <m>y = <eval obj="b"/>v</m>,
        <m>z = <eval obj="c"/>w</m>, which carries <m>E</m> to the unit
        ball. What is the Jacobian determinant
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y,z)}{\partial(u,v,w)}</m>?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y,z)}{\partial(u,v,w)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="8"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Evaluate the triple integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_{E}
        \left(\frac{x^2}{<eval obj="a"/>^2}
        + \frac{y^2}{<eval obj="b"/>^2}
        + \frac{z^2}{<eval obj="c"/>^2}\right) dx\,dy\,dz =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Setting <m>x = 2u</m>, <m>y = 3v</m>,
        <m>z = 4w</m> carries <m>E</m> to the unit ball and
        has Jacobian <m>24</m>, while the integrand becomes
        <m>u^2+v^2+w^2 = \rho^2</m>. Hence
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_E \left(\frac{x^2}{2^2}
        + \frac{y^2}{3^2}
        + \frac{z^2}{4^2}\right) dV
        = 24\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^{1}
          \rho^4 \sin\phi \,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = \frac{4\,\pi\,24}{5} .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here the Jacobian is <m>(2)(3)(4) = 24</m>, so the integral is
<m>\dfrac{4\pi(24)}{5} = \dfrac{96\pi}{5} \approx 60.32</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 5.  Centre of mass of a tetrahedron  (RP3 #5)                       -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-tetrahedron-com">
    <title>Centre of Mass of a Tetrahedron</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="k" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="15" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="mass" context="number">
        <de-expression>8*k/105</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xcm" context="number">
        <de-expression>2/9</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ycm" context="number">
        <de-expression>2/9</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zcm" context="number">
        <de-expression>1/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the centre of mass of the solid <m>W</m> bounded by the planes
        <m>x + y + z = 1</m>, <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, and <m>z = 0</m>,
        assuming a mass density of
        <m>\rho(x,y,z) = <eval obj="k"/>\sqrt{z}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Find the mass of the solid.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iiint_W <eval obj="k"/>\sqrt{z}\,dV =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="mass" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the coordinates of the centre of mass.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>x_{\mathrm{CM}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xcm" width="10"/>,
        <m>y_{\mathrm{CM}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="ycm" width="10"/>,
        <m>z_{\mathrm{CM}} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="zcm" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Describe <m>W</m> by <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 1-x</m>,
        <m>0 \le z \le 1-x-y</m>. Then
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_W 6\sqrt{z}\,dV
        = \frac{8\left(6\right)}{105}, \qquad
        \iiint_W 6x\sqrt{z}\,dV
        = \frac{16\left(6\right)}{945}, \qquad
        \iiint_W 6z\sqrt{z}\,dV
        = \frac{8\left(6\right)}{315},
      </me>
      <p>
        and the <m>y</m>-numerator equals the <m>x</m>-numerator by the
        symmetry of <m>W</m> and of <m>\rho</m> in <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
        Dividing,
      </p>
      <me>
        \left(x_{\mathrm{CM}}, y_{\mathrm{CM}}, z_{\mathrm{CM}}\right)
        = \left(\frac29, \frac29, \frac13\right),
      </me>
      <p>
        independently of the constant <m>6</m>, which cancels
        from every ratio.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>k = 6</m>, so the mass is <m>\dfrac{8(6)}{105} = \dfrac{16}{35}
\approx 0.457</m>, and the centre of mass is
<m>\left(\tfrac29, \tfrac29, \tfrac13\right)</m>, independent of
<m>k</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 6.  Mass and centre of mass of a lamina  (RP3 #6)                   -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-lamina-rectangle">
    <title>Mass and Centre of Mass of a Rectangular Lamina</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="A" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="B" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="C" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="D" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="E" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="mass" context="number">
        <de-expression>A^2*B*C/2+B^2*D*A/2+E*A*B</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xmom" context="number">
        <de-expression>C*A^3*B/3+D*A^2*B^2/4+E*A^2*B/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ymom" context="number">
        <de-expression>C*A^2*B^2/4+D*A*B^3/3+E*A*B^2/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xbar" context="number">
        <de-expression>xmom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ybar" context="number">
        <de-expression>ymom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A lamina occupies the part of the rectangle
        <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="A"/></m>,
        <m>0 \le y \le <eval obj="B"/></m>, and the density at each point is
        given by the function
        <m>\rho(x,y) = <eval obj="C"/>x + <eval obj="D"/>y + <eval obj="E"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> What is the total mass?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>m =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="mass" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Where is the centre of mass?
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\bar{x} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xbar" width="12"/>,
        <m>\bar{y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="ybar" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Over the rectangle <m>R</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        m = \int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{3}
        \left(4x + 5y + 6\right)
        dy\,dx = 105 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        and the two moments are
        <m>\iint_R x\rho\,dA = 113</m> and
        <m>\iint_R y\rho\,dA = 180</m>. Dividing each by the
        mass gives <m>\bar{x} \approx 1.076</m> and
        <m>\bar{y} \approx 1.714</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>A = 2</m>, <m>B = 3</m>, <m>C = 4</m>, <m>D = 5</m> and
<m>E = 6</m>, so <m>m = 105</m>, the moments are <m>113</m> and
<m>180</m>, and
<m>\left(\bar x, \bar y\right)
= \left(\tfrac{113}{105}, \tfrac{12}{7}\right)
\approx (1.076, 1.714)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 7.  Volume between a sphere and a tangent sphere  (RP3 #7)          -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-sphere-minus-sphere">
    <title>Volume Between Two Spheres in Spherical Coordinates</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="volume" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*a^3/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the volume of the region outside the sphere
        <m>\rho = <eval obj="a"/>\cos\phi</m> and inside the half sphere
        <m>\rho = <eval obj="a"/></m>, with
        <m>\phi \in \left[0, \frac{\pi}{2}\right]</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Volume <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="volume" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        In spherical coordinates,
      </p>
      <me>
        V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!
        \int_{4\cos\phi}^{4}
        \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = \frac{2\pi\left(4\right)^3}{3}
        \int_0^{\pi/2}\left(1 - \cos^3\phi\right)\sin\phi\,d\phi .
      </me>
      <p>
        With <m>c = \cos\phi</m> the remaining integral is
        <m>\int_0^1 \left(1 - c^3\right) dc = \tfrac34</m>, so
        <m>V = \frac{2\pi\left(4\right)^3}{3}\cdot\frac34
        = \frac{\pi\left(4\right)^3}{2}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 4</m>, so the volume is
<m>\dfrac{\pi\left(4^3\right)}{2} = 32\pi \approx 100.53</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 8.  A spherical triple integral over a shell  (RP3 #8)              -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-spherical-shell">
    <title>A Triple Integral Over a Spherical Shell</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="r0" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="gap" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r1" context="number">
        <de-expression>r0+gap</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="radial" context="number">
        <de-expression>(r1^3-r0^3)/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>pi*(r1^3-r0^3)/3</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate, in spherical coordinates, the triple integral of
        <m>f(\rho,\theta,\phi) = \cos\phi</m> over the region
        <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>,
        <m>0 \le \phi \le \frac{\pi}{2}</m>,
        <m><eval obj="r0"/> \le \rho \le <eval obj="r1"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> The <m>\rho</m>-integral separates from the
        rest. Evaluate it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_{<eval obj="r0"/>}^{<eval obj="r1"/>}
        \rho^2\,d\rho =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="radial" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Evaluate the whole integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integral <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Because the integrand and the volume element both factor, the
        triple integral splits into a product:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!
        \int_{1}^{3}
        \left(\cos\phi\right)\rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = \left(\int_0^{2\pi} d\theta\right)
          \left(\int_{1}^{3}\rho^2 d\rho\right)
          \left(\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos\phi\sin\phi\,d\phi\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        These are <m>2\pi</m>,
        <m>\frac{\left(3\right)^3
        - \left(1\right)^3}{3}</m>, and <m>\tfrac12</m>,
        so the integral equals
        <m>\frac{\pi\left(\left(3\right)^3
        - \left(1\right)^3\right)}{3}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here the radial integral is <m>\dfrac{3^3 - 1^3}{3} = \dfrac{26}{3}</m>,
so the triple integral is <m>\dfrac{26\pi}{3} \approx 27.23</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 9.  A Gaussian-type integral between two spheres  (RP3 #9)          -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-gaussian-shell">
    <title>An Exponential Integral Between Two Spheres</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="2" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="gap" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-expression>a+gap</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="asq" context="number">
        <de-expression>a^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="bsq" context="number">
        <de-expression>b^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>2*pi*(exp(-asq)-exp(-bsq))</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use spherical coordinates to evaluate the triple integral
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_E \frac{e^{-\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)}}
        {\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}}\,dV ,
      </me>
      <p>
        where <m>E</m> is the region bounded by the spheres
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = <eval obj="asq"/></m> and
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = <eval obj="bsq"/></m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Answer <m>=</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        The spheres have radii <m>1</m> and
        <m>2</m>. In spherical coordinates,
      </p>
      <me>
        \iiint_E \frac{e^{-\rho^2}}{\rho}\,dV
        = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!
          \int_{1}^{2}
          \frac{e^{-\rho^2}}{\rho}\,\rho^2\sin\phi
          \,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
        = 4\pi\int_{1}^{2}
          \rho\,e^{-\rho^2}\,d\rho .
      </me>
      <p>
        With <m>w = \rho^2</m> this is
        <m>4\pi\left[-\tfrac12 e^{-\rho^2}\right]_{1}^{2}
        = 2\pi\left(e^{-1} - e^{-4}\right)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here the radii are <m>1</m> and <m>2</m>, so the integral is
<m>2\pi\left(e^{-1} - e^{-4}\right) \approx 2.196</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 10.  Jacobian of a linear transformation  (RP3 #10)                 -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-linear-jacobian">
    <title>The Jacobian of a Linear Transformation</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="p" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="q" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="3" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="s" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="5" max="9" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="det" context="number">
        <de-expression>p*s-q*r</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xu" context="number">
        <de-expression>p</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xv" context="number">
        <de-expression>q</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yu" context="number">
        <de-expression>r</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="yv" context="number">
        <de-expression>s</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the transformation <m>T</m> from <m>uv</m>-coordinates to
        <m>xy</m>-coordinates given by
      </p>
      <me>
        T(u,v) = \left\langle <eval obj="p"/>u + <eval obj="q"/>v, \;
        <eval obj="r"/>u + <eval obj="s"/>v \right\rangle .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Find the four partial derivatives.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="xu" width="6"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial x}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xv" width="6"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial u} =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="yu" width="6"/>,
        <m>\dfrac{\partial y}{\partial v} =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="yv" width="6"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Compute the Jacobian of this transformation.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="det" width="10"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        For a linear transformation the partial derivatives are just the
        coefficients, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \mathrm{Jac}(T) = \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
        = \begin{vmatrix}
          7 &amp; 2 \\
          3 &amp; 8
          \end{vmatrix}
        = \left(7\right)\left(8\right)
        - \left(2\right)\left(3\right)
        = 50 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        a constant: a linear map scales every area by the same factor.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here the partial derivatives are <m>7</m>, <m>2</m>, <m>3</m> and
<m>8</m>, so the Jacobian is <m>(7)(8) - (2)(3) = 50</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 11.  The map T(u,v) = (u^2-v^2, 2uv)  (RP3 #11)                     -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-quadratic-map">
    <title>A Quadratic Map and the Integral It Simplifies</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="a" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="fuv" context="formula">
        <de-expression>u^2+v^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="jac" context="formula">
        <de-expression>4*(u^2+v^2)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="value" context="number">
        <de-expression>56*a^6/45</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The map <m>T(u,v) = \left(u^2-v^2,\; 2uv\right)</m> carries the
        triangle
        <m>D_0 = \left\{(u,v) : 0 \le v \le u \le <eval obj="a"/>\right\}</m>
        onto the domain <m>D</m> in the first quadrant bounded by
        <m>x = 0</m>, <m>y = 0</m>, and the parabola through the image of
        the edge <m>u = <eval obj="a"/></m>. Use <m>T</m> to evaluate
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_{D} \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Part A.</term> Express the integrand in terms of <m>u</m> and
        <m>v</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\sqrt{x^2+y^2} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="fuv" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part B.</term> Find the Jacobian of <m>T</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\dfrac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="jac" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Part C.</term> Evaluate the integral.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\iint_{D} \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="value" width="14"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        Since <m>x = u^2-v^2</m> and <m>y = 2uv</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \sqrt{x^2+y^2}
        = \sqrt{\left(u^2+v^2\right)^2} = u^2+v^2, \qquad
        \frac{\partial(x,y)}{\partial(u,v)}
        = \begin{vmatrix} 2u &amp; -2v \\ 2v &amp; 2u \end{vmatrix}
        = 4\left(u^2+v^2\right).
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore
      </p>
      <me>
        \iint_D \sqrt{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy
        = 4\int_0^{2}\!\!\int_0^{u}
          \left(u^4 + 2u^2v^2 + v^4\right) dv\,du
        = 4\int_0^{2} \frac{28}{15}u^5\,du
        = \frac{56\left(2\right)^6}{45} .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>a = 2</m>, so the integral equals
<m>\dfrac{56\left(2^6\right)}{45} = \dfrac{3584}{45} \approx 79.64</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 12.  Centre of gravity of a region between two curves  (RP3 #12)    -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-centre-of-gravity">
    <title>Centre of Gravity of a Region Between Two Curves</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="m" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="1" max="5" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="b" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="4" max="10" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="zero" context="number">
        <de-expression>0</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xstar" context="number">
        <de-expression>(-m+sqrt(m^2+4*b))/2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="lower" context="formula">
        <de-expression>m*x</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="upper" context="formula">
        <de-expression>b-x^2</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="mass" context="number">
        <de-expression>xstar^6/12+(-b-m^2/2)*xstar^4/4+b^2*xstar^2/4</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xmom" context="number">
        <de-expression>xstar^7/14+(-b-m^2/2)*xstar^5/5+b^2*xstar^3/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ymom" context="number">
        <de-expression>-xstar^8/24+b*xstar^6/6-m^3*xstar^5/15-b^2*xstar^4/4+b^3*xstar^2/6</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="xbar" context="number">
        <de-expression>xmom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="ybar" context="number">
        <de-expression>ymom/mass</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The region <m>R</m> is bounded by the curves
        <m>y = <eval obj="m"/>x</m>, <m>y = <eval obj="b"/> - x^2</m>, and
        the <m>y</m>-axis, and its mass density is
        <m>\delta(x,y) = xy</m>. To find the centre of gravity of <m>R</m>
        you would compute the three integrals
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        \delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>,
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        x\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>, and
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        y\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx</m>, where
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>c =</m> <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="zero" width="6"/>,
        <m>d =</m> <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="xstar" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>p(x) =</m>
        <fillin name="b3" mode="math" ansobj="lower" width="12"/>,
        <m>q(x) =</m>
        <fillin name="b4" mode="math" ansobj="upper" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        \delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx =</m>
        <fillin name="b5" mode="math" ansobj="mass" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        x\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx =</m>
        <fillin name="b6" mode="math" ansobj="xmom" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\displaystyle\int_c^d\!\!\int_{p(x)}^{q(x)}
        y\,\delta(x,y)\,dy\,dx =</m>
        <fillin name="b7" mode="math" ansobj="ymom" width="14"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        and finally the centre of gravity is
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\overline{x} =</m>
        <fillin name="b8" mode="math" ansobj="xbar" width="12"/>,
        <m>\overline{y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b9" mode="math" ansobj="ybar" width="12"/>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        The line and the parabola meet where
        <m>x^2 + 2x - 8 = 0</m>, that is at
        <m>x = 2</m>, so <m>c = 0</m> and
        <m>d = 2</m>, with
        <m>p(x) = 2x</m> and
        <m>q(x) = 8 - x^2</m>. Carrying out the three
        integrals of <m>xy</m>, <m>x^2y</m>, and <m>xy^2</m> over that
        region gives <m>\tfrac{88}{3}</m>, <m>30.476</m>,
        and <m>142.933</m> respectively, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \overline{x} = \frac{30.476}{\tfrac{88}{3}}
        \approx 1.039, \qquad
        \overline{y} = \frac{142.933}{\tfrac{88}{3}}
        \approx 4.873 .
      </me>
      <p>
        Here <m>m = 2</m> and <m>b = 8</m>, so <m>x^{*} = 2</m> exactly, giving
<m>c = 0</m>, <m>d = 2</m>, <m>p(x) = 2x</m>, <m>q(x) = 8 - x^2</m>, the
three integrals <m>\tfrac{88}{3} \approx 29.33</m>, <m>30.48</m> and
<m>142.93</m>, and
<m>\left(\overline{x}, \overline{y}\right) \approx (1.039, 4.873)</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- 13.  Centre of mass of a quarter disc  (RP3 #13)                    -->
  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-r3p-quarter-disc-com">
    <title>Centre of Mass of a Quarter Disc</title>
    <setup>
      <de-object name="r" context="number">
        <de-random distribution="discrete" min="2" max="8" by="1"/>
      </de-object>
      <de-object name="coord" context="number">
        <de-expression>4*r/(3*pi)</de-expression>
      </de-object>
    </setup>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The centre of mass of the quarter disc of radius
        <m><eval obj="r"/></m> lying under
        <m>y = \sqrt{\left(<eval obj="r"/>\right)^2 - x^2}</m> for
        <m>0 \le x \le <eval obj="r"/></m>, with constant density, is a
        point <m>P = \left(\overline{x}, \overline{y}\right)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>\overline{x} =</m>
        <fillin name="b1" mode="math" ansobj="coord" width="12"/>,
        <m>\overline{y} =</m>
        <fillin name="b2" mode="math" ansobj="coord" width="12"/>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Hint.</em> Use symmetry.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <evaluation>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
      <evaluate>
        <test correct="yes">
          <mathcmp use-answer="yes"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
        <test>
          <numcmp min="-1000000000" max="1000000000"/>
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </test>
      </evaluate>
    </evaluation>
      <solution component="review3-solutions">
      <p>
        By symmetry about the line <m>y = x</m>, the centre of mass lies on
        that line, so <m>\overline{x} = \overline{y}</m>. Computing
        <m>\overline{x}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        \overline{x}
        = \frac{\displaystyle\int_0^{3}
          x\sqrt{\left(3\right)^2-x^2}\,dx}
          {\displaystyle\frac{\pi\left(3\right)^2}{4}}
        = \frac{\left(3\right)^3/3}
          {\pi\left(3\right)^2/4}
        = \frac{4\left(3\right)}{3\pi}
        \approx \tfrac{4}{\pi} \approx 1.273 ,
      </me>
      <p>
        and <m>\overline{y}</m> has the same value.
      </p>
      <p>
        Here <m>r = 3</m>, so
<m>\overline{x} = \overline{y} = \dfrac{4(3)}{3\pi} = \dfrac{4}{\pi}
\approx 1.273</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
